{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CfyDoiKC8yCaVqvrZeUuV","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01DtL1CrBJM3YGCm6DGo4SQa","name":"StructuredOutput","input":{"answers":[{"question_id":"Q004_ferritin_like_functional_divergence","answer":"In SCOPe 2.08, ferritins (a.25.1.1, sunid 47241), ribonucleotide reductase-like proteins (a.25.1.2, sunid 47253; the R2 di-iron subunit family) and manganese catalase/T-catalase (a.25.1.3, sunid 100951) are three separate FAMILIES. They sit in one SUPERFAMILY, a.25.1 'Ferritin-like' (sunid 47240), which is in the fold a.25 'Ferritin-like' (sunid 47239), class a 'All alpha proteins'. SCOPe groups them as a superfamily because of structural and functional evidence of a probable common evolutionary origin. The shared feature is a four-helix-bundle core that binds a carboxylate-bridged dinuclear metal site (the di-iron/di-manganese site). Sequence similarity between the families is low, so they are distinct families rather than close homologs. The right reading is remote homology that has diverged in function. The same scaffold has been adapted to different chemistry: ferritin is an Fe(II) ferroxidase that stores iron; RNR R2 forms a diferric-tyrosyl radical for nucleotide reduction; Mn catalase uses a Mn2 site for H2O2 disproportionation. The superfamily also holds other families, such as half-ferritin, YciF-like, MiaE-like, Rv2844-like, PMT1231-like and AMB4284-like. Superfamily membership supports a shared ancestry and a conserved scaffold and metal-binding architecture. It does not support transferring function, metal specificity, redox chemistry, catalytic mechanism, oligomeric state or substrate from one family to another. Function has to be assigned from family-level evidence: conserved ligand residues, the specific metal, and experimental data. For modeling or template use, prefer templates from the same family. A template from another family is useful for the helical core, but not for active-site chemistry or quaternary assembly. A shared fold alone would not establish homology. Here the superfamily level, not just the fold, carries the homology claim, and it comes from curated SCOPe classification, not from my own sequence or structure analysis in this answer. The family labels are ferritin, RNR-like and Mn catalase. They record different chemistries, and they do not contradict the single shared superfamily.","evidence_used":["Pretrained knowledge of ferritin-like di-metal carboxylate proteins","mcp__scope__get_scop_node","mcp__scope__list_scop_children","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sccs/a.25.1","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sccs/a.25.1/parents","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sccs/a.25.1/children"]}]},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3315,"cache_read_input_tokens":18803,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":3315},"output_tokens":20,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f92a805c-0094-4ebc-89fb-ffcd7b44f133","uuid":"cde16a07-c3ca-43bf-829a-87ae525c127d","timestamp":"2026-10-12T23:36:45.115Z","request_id":"req_011CfyDoi4ZyLMJD9EXQ8Z2H","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01DtL1CrBJM3YGCm6DGo4SQa":{"answers":[{"question_id":"Q004_ferritin_like_functional_divergence","answer":"In SCOPe 2.08, ferritins (a.25.1.1, sunid 47241), ribonucleotide reductase-like proteins (a.25.1.2, sunid 47253; the R2 di-iron subunit family) and manganese catalase/T-catalase (a.25.1.3, sunid 100951) are three separate FAMILIES. They sit in one SUPERFAMILY, a.25.1 'Ferritin-like' (sunid 47240), which is in the fold a.25 'Ferritin-like' (sunid 47239), class a 'All alpha proteins'. SCOPe groups them as a superfamily because of structural and functional evidence of a probable common evol